---TITLE---
RankCal: Counterfactual Influence Quantization for Action-Conditioned Latent World-Model Planning
---HOOK---
At matched memory and nominal bit-width, candidate-order agreement should improve more by protecting sites with high rollout-ranking influence than by protecting sites with the largest local reconstruction error, especially in the longer-horizon cells of the evaluation grid. This predicts that a deliberately worse tensor-MSE allocation can yield higher closed-loop success, exposing a decision-level failure mode that the full-text DINO-WM sweep did not measure.
---CORE_MECHANISM---
Freeze a public DreamerV3- or TD-MPC2-style action-conditioned latent world model at FP16 (an inferred pilot anchor, not a prior claim or a new backbone), add no foundation-model pretraining, and use the existing uniform-affine PyTorch torch.ao.quantization path as the numerical weight/activation PTQ operator. Let G be the deterministic set of every eligible Linear or Conv weight tensor and its activation output reached by the planner's scoring forward path; optimizer state and planner-only code are not quantized, and each g has a measured peak-byte cost m_g(b). From named DeepMind Control Suite (DMC) visual continuous-control tasks, collect disjoint self-built calibration and evaluation streams, with each record r storing the model-internal latent z_t, a planner-generated candidate set U_t, and its horizon, update schedule, receding-horizon convention, and tie rule. Use the pre-registered grid H={floor(H_native/2), H_native, min(2H_native,H_episode)}, N={max(1,floor(N_native/2)), N_native, 2N_native}, M={max(1,floor(M0/4)), max(1,floor(M0/2)), M0}, S={s_native, random shooting, CEM}, E={e_native, one-step}, and T={stable-index, seeded-random}, removing duplicate cells; M0 is the largest per-cell trajectory count fitting the fixed A100 pilot envelope, and grid-cell weights omega_c default to 1/|C| unless deployment frequencies are preregistered. For each g and allowed bit pair b in B={(FP16,FP16), W8A8, W8A4, W4A8, W4A4}, quantize only g with the same observer/calibration data, keep every other site FP16, rerun the identical candidate set under cell c, and define d_g,c(r;b) as the normalized pairwise candidate-order disagreement from FP16 with ties resolved by tau. The central object is the rollout-ranking influence profile I_g,c(b)=average_r in D_c of d_g,c(r;b) and I_g(b)=sum_c omega_c I_g,c(b), a dimensionless counterfactual estimand of how much site g changes planner ordering over the observed rollout regime; fixed-size per-cell accumulators compute this mean exactly for the stated site-only estimand rather than approximating an unchosen Shapley value. The heterogeneous companion partitions sites by their full vector (I_g,c(b)) across cells and chooses b_g by minimizing sum_g omega_g I_g(b_g) subject to sum_g m_g(b_g) <= M_mem, where omega_g defaults to 1/|G| and M_mem is swept over the Pareto range from an FP16-feasible target to the lowest supported bit mix. The resulting all-sites assignment is evaluated end-to-end on disjoint episodes for every grid cell and memory target, reporting rank agreement, first-action agreement, closed-loop return or success, peak memory, and control frequency; the influence profile is therefore an empirical allocation signal rather than a claim of global optimality. A matched intervention keeps candidate pools, start states, bit histogram, memory target, planner schedule, receding-horizon convention, and tie rule fixed while randomizing the mapping from I_g to bit pairs, so any downstream advantage must come from the computed decision influence rather than bit count or local tensor error. The evidence boundary is explicit: arxiv:2602.02110v1 is connector-verified host_recall with fulltext H/html_arxiv, arxiv:2608.24855v1 is arxiv-retrieved with fulltext T3/html_arxiv, and arxiv:2607.28405v1 is connector-verified host_recall but has no fulltext cache entry in this run and is used only as an abstract-only adjacent comparison; none is introduced as the backbone.
---CORE_MECHANISM_REASONING---
BLOCK 1 — PREMISES ledger
P1. Candidate action-sequence ordering is the operational decision variable, not merely a proxy for latent or observation reconstruction — reason: the planner selects actions by comparing candidate scores.
P2. A site-only quantization intervention with the candidate pool, FP16 weights elsewhere, and planner inputs held fixed isolates a useful marginal decision influence (untested — falsification target) — reason: it creates a matched counterfactual while preserving the downstream scoring path.
P3. The influence profile transfers sufficiently from FP16 planner visitation to quantized receding-horizon visitation for allocation to help (untested — falsification target) — reason: the calibration stream is generated by the same control loop, but low-bit errors can move later states off that stream.
P4. Site-level marginal influence is sufficiently compositional for a memory-constrained assignment to be useful even though joint quantization can interact (untested — falsification target) — reason: the single-site table makes the pilot tractable, while the all-sites evaluation tests the approximation.
P5. Existing public weights expose the eligible weight tensors and activation outputs needed for numerical PTQ, and the chosen DMC model/planner path can be run at FP16 and mixed W/A precision — reason: the proposal uses model-internal access, not gradients, closed-model APIs, or a new training corpus.
OBSERVATION-MODEL: D_cal is sampled as stratified trajectories from named DMC tasks under the FP16 native, random-shooting, and CEM schedules, with fixed start-state and random-seed manifests; it is not unbiased over all reachable latent states, is biased toward the FP16 planner's visitation and candidate distribution, and is kept disjoint from D_eval.
ESTIMAND: for each site g and bit pair b, I_g(b) estimates the expected normalized pairwise candidate-order discordance between the FP16 planner and the model with only g quantized, over the uniform-weighted cells C and the records D_c drawn from this stated DMC/planner process; it is conditional on that process, not an unbiased population average over all states, tasks, or backbones.
BLOCK 2 — NAIVE-BASELINE AUDIT
Naive version: run uniform-affine PTQ, select each site's weight and activation bits by lowest layer-output reconstruction error on short calibration trajectories, and spend the remaining memory budget on the sites with the smallest local error.
It fails under branch (i): this version silently assumes that local reconstruction and calibration-distribution fidelity predict candidate ranking under recurrent action-conditioned rollouts, a premise that is false as a general rule because planner-induced states and candidate orderings change with horizon. RankCal confronts that failure with site-only counterfactual ranking influence; although counterfactual intervention is a standard diagnostic tool, the domain-specific structure is the adaptive candidate pool plus receding-horizon latent feedback under a fixed mixed W/A memory budget, so this is an application-grade method rather than a claim of a new generic causal estimator.
BLOCK 3 — DESIGN RATIONALE
I kept one anchor and deferred the cross-backbone transfer gap and the standalone local-proxy audit because the former is a validation axis and the latter is a negative-control comparison that can run through the same anchor machinery; adding either as a separate leg would make the paper broader without making the method more complete.
I selected C00 from the anchor alternates because its fixed-model counterfactual measurement object directly matches candidate-order influence, while the heterogeneous companion turns that object into a runnable memory-constrained W/A allocation.
The method's central object is defined as a site-isolated estimand and the final full-model evaluation, not a claimed Shapley or globally additive optimum; this keeps the C00 compression step honest when joint quantization interactions violate additivity.
I rejected uniform local-reconstruction, generic activation-sensitivity, and module/horizon-only routes because they regress to the established n0-n2 PTQ lineage or repeat the n3 DINO-WM sweep; DINO-WM and LeFlow are full-text comparators, QuantWAMs is connector-verified but abstract-only and adjacent, and DreamerV3/TD-MPC2 remain explicitly inferred pilot anchors.
---CORE_MECHANISM_STEPS---
STEP[1] type=String
"1. Select one publicly available DreamerV3- or TD-MPC2-style action-conditioned latent world-model checkpoint as the inferred pilot anchor and one named DeepMind Control Suite task family; freeze its FP16 weights, reproduce the native planner path, and write a reference configuration containing H_native, N_native, s_native, e_native, and the native tie rule. Inputs: public checkpoint and DMC environment. Outputs: reproducible FP16 model, planner, and configuration; no DINO-WM or LeWM backbone is introduced.\n2. Trace the PyTorch scoring forward graph and enumerate G as the eligible Linear/Conv weight tensors plus their activation outputs; attach the existing uniform-affine observers without changing the FP16 path. Inputs: frozen model and planner code. Outputs: site manifest, per-site weight/activation access, and the FP16 peak-memory map; FP16 planner scores remain unchanged.\n3. Build disjoint D_cal and D_eval streams from the named DMC tasks using the grid C over M, N, S, H, E, and T; for each calibration record save z_t, the FP16-generated U_t, and the cell metadata, while D_eval contains fresh environment episodes. Inputs: DMC, FP16 planner, M0 selection rule, and fixed seed manifest. Outputs: stratified calibration records and held-out episodes with no shared episode identifiers.\n4. For every g, b, and c, run site-only PTQ using the same observer data, replace only g in the FP16 checkpoint, score the identical U_t, and calculate d_g,c; the intervention propagates through every later latent transition and planner score, so its output is a changed candidate ranking and possibly a changed first action, not just a changed tensor statistic. Inputs: site manifest and D_cal. Outputs: cell-level rank-disagreement tables.\n5. Aggregate the tables into I_g,c(b) and I_g(b), retain the full profile vector across C, and compute local layer-output error and calibration-distribution fidelity only as named comparison signals. Inputs: rank-disagreement tables and omega_c. Outputs: the counterfactual influence profile and comparator table used by the allocator.\n6. For each M_mem in the pre-registered Pareto memory sweep, partition sites by their profile vectors and solve the stated memory-constrained assignment over B with the measured m_g and default omega_g. Inputs: I_g,c(b), m_g(b), B, omega_g, and M_mem. Outputs: a per-site weight/activation bit map; materializing that map changes which numerical operators feed future latent rollouts and therefore which action sequence the planner selects.\n7. Materialize the all-sites mixed-precision checkpoint and run the full planner end-to-end for every c and M_mem on D_eval, allowing the selected candidate set and receding-horizon execution to follow each cell's declared convention. Inputs: FP16 checkpoint, bit map, and fresh episodes. Outputs: candidate-order agreement, first-action agreement, closed-loop return or success, peak memory, and control-frequency measurements.\n8. Run matched negative controls by randomizing the mapping from I_g to bit pairs and by using the local-reconstruction allocator while preserving the same bit histogram, M_mem, candidate/update grid, receding-horizon convention, tie rule, and evaluation episodes. Inputs: the same D_eval episodes and matched resource budget. Outputs: downstream control metrics that test whether the profile, rather than resource amount alone, determines the benefit.\n9. Report the full memory-versus-control-fidelity Pareto curves and the result of the randomized mapping; choose a deployment map only by the requested M_mem target and retain all grid cells in the paper's claim, rather than selecting a favorable horizon, calibration size, planner schedule, execution convention, or tie rule after observing outcomes. Inputs: all evaluation and control tables. Outputs: an auditable method specification and falsifiable claim."

