g, b, and c, run site-only PTQ using the same observer data, replace only g in the FP16 checkpoint, and score the identical U_t through the declared imagined latent rollout. Let K_r=|U_t|; for K_r >= 2 set d_g,c(r;b)=[sum_{i<j} 1{ord_tau(s_g,b(u_i),s_g,b(u_j)) != ord_tau(s_FP16(u_i),s_FP16(u_j))}]/binom(K_r,2), where ord_tau treats scores within tau as a tie and resolves it by candidate index; for K_r < 2 set d_g,c(r;b)=0 and record the zero-pair count. The intervention propagates through every later latent transition and planner score, so its output is a changed candidate ranking and possibly a changed first action, not just a changed tensor statistic. Inputs: site manifest and D_cal. Outputs: cell-level rank-disagreement tables.\n5. Aggregate the tables into I_g,c(b) and I_g(b), retain the full profile vector across C, and compute local layer-output error and calibration-distribution fidelity only as named comparison signals. Inputs: rank-disagreement tables and omega_c. Outputs: the finite-sample counterfactual influence profile and comparator table used by the allocator.\n6. For each feasible M_mem in the pre-registered Pareto memory sweep, retain the full profile vector as the heterogeneity representation and solve the stated memory-constrained assignment over B_deploy with the measured system-level PeakBytes(b_1,...,b_|G|) <= M_mem; if no assignment is feasible, mark that cell/target infeasible and report no downstream metric rather than silently substituting another map. Inputs: I_g,c(b), PeakBytes, B_deploy, omega_g, and M_mem. Outputs: a per-site weight/activation bit map; materializing that map changes which numerical operators feed future latent rollouts and therefore which action sequence the planner selects.\n7. Materialize the all-sites mixed-precision checkpoint and run the full planner end-to-end for every feasible c and M_mem on D_eval, allowing the selected candidate set and receding-horizon execution to follow each cell's declared convention. Inputs: FP16 checkpoint, bit map, and fresh episodes. Outputs: candidate-order agreement, first-action agreement, closed-loop return or success, peak memory, and control-frequency measurements.\n8. Run matched negative controls by sampling non-identity assignments with the same bit-pair histogram and exact measured PeakBytes as the RankCal assignment, and by using the local-reconstruction allocator while preserving the same M_mem, candidate/update grid, receding-horizon convention, tie rule, planner seeds, and evaluation episodes. If no non-identity assignment satisfies both matches, record the control as unavailable; interpret an observed difference as a matched diagnostic, not a proof that I_g is the sole causal factor. Inputs: the same D_eval episodes and matched resource budget. Outputs: downstream control metrics that test whether the profile, rather than resource amount alone, determines the benefit.\n9. Report the full memory-versus-control-fidelity Pareto curves and the result of the randomized mapping; choose a deployment map only by the requested feasible M_mem target and retain all feasible grid cells in the paper's claim, rather than selecting a favorable horizon, calibration size, planner schedule, execution convention, or tie rule after observing outcomes. Inputs: all evaluation and control tables. Outputs: an auditable method specification and falsifiable claim.",
